Rym: Well, the, the worst part is the pan doodle briefly showed us a vision of a better America because there was a brief time where I could work from home and it was fine and just worked and was reasonable.

188
00:15:53,820 --> 00:15:59,030
Rym: While simultaneously I could just go to a government website, click, I would like a vaccine.

189
00:15:59,331 --> 00:16:04,400
Rym: And then they would say, go to this place and you'll just get a vaccine, no paperwork, no insurance, no nothing.

190
00:16:05,521 --> 00:16:07,006
Rym: And all that's, both of those are gone.

191
00:16:07,327 --> 00:16:07,869
Rym: Everything's gone.

192
00:16:07,889 --> 00:16:11,180
Rym: We lost, we lost everything we even gained during the pandemic.

193
00:16:12,522 --> 00:16:14,780
Scott: Except for the attitude of people not wanting to work.

194
00:16:15,240 --> 00:16:15,521
Rym: Oh yeah.

195
00:16:17,364 --> 00:16:26,120
Rym: I think that attitude is due to partly a fraying at the edges of society that's starting to intrude into what's left of the middle class, but that's a little bit beyond a tech news roundup.

196
00:16:26,361 --> 00:16:31,600
Rym: So, uh, in other news, uh, this is actually kind of a fascinating one.

197
00:16:32,000 --> 00:16:44,180
Rym: The US Surgeon General has stated that social media as a concept, like social media apps and websites should have warnings, just like cigarettes and alcohol have warnings about potential effects.

198
00:16:44,940 --> 00:16:45,806
Rym: I don't disagree.

199
00:16:45,826 --> 00:16:55,060
Scott: I think information has- There are many studies showing that, you know, people who use it too much, especially children, uh, you know, it fucks them up psychologically, right?

200
00:16:56,521 --> 00:16:59,006
Scott: Um, there's also lots of anecdotal evidence, right?

201
00:16:59,287 --> 00:17:05,339
Scott: I read a story the other day, obviously just an anecdote, but some parent, right, had a kid.

202
00:17:06,020 --> 00:17:13,619
Scott: And when the kid, you know, was doing too much social media, they were really going down some dark, dark paths, right?

203
00:17:13,980 --> 00:17:21,140
Scott: And when the dad successfully took away the phone, the kid, he noticed like a notable improvement in the kid's life, right?

204
00:17:21,621 --> 00:17:27,640
Scott: And then when the kid was secretly hiding, you know, then the kid took a downturn, secretly hiding a phone somewhere, you know?

205
00:17:28,099 --> 00:17:40,080
Scott: So it's like, you know, she was concentrating more on, you know, gaining the approval of peers and focusing all her time and energy on that instead of studying and what you're supposed to be doing when you're a kid.

206
00:17:40,981 --> 00:17:52,920
Rym: Yeah, but I think what's pernicious about it is that a lot, like kind of like with the total unrestricted internet, the internet, there's a lot of kids who are just totally fine as long as they have a decent upbringing and are reasonably intelligent and other factors aren't at play.

207
00:17:53,460 --> 00:18:03,580
Rym: Like they're just fine, but a subset of the population for a variety of reasons just gets absolutely wrecked by this kind of connectivity.

208
00:18:03,700 --> 00:18:13,920
Rym: And I think it's getting exacerbated specifically and almost solely by the algorithmic engagement drives and the fact that these are increasingly also becoming platforms for state-sponsored propaganda.

209
00:18:15,681 --> 00:18:18,385
Scott: The state-sponsored propaganda is causing the teen suicides.

210
00:18:19,067 --> 00:18:26,580
Scott: I'm pretty sure it's the cyberbullying and the, you know, the messaging each other and, you know, right?

211
00:18:27,040 --> 00:18:29,960
Scott: All your friends posting things and whatnot.

212
00:18:30,542 --> 00:18:36,120
Rym: Well, but there's also, there's the recruitment wing into basically right wing circles of primarily bullying kids.

213
00:18:36,160 --> 00:18:40,367
Scott: That is a thing that exists, but that is not the, that is not the reason the Surgeon General cares.

214
00:18:40,407 --> 00:18:48,220
Scott: The Surgeon General cares is because there are studies showing that, yeah, teens get higher rates of anxiety, depression and suicide when they're on social media too much.

215
00:18:49,360 --> 00:18:52,920
Scott: And it has nothing to do with the state-sponsored propaganda.

216
00:18:53,621 --> 00:18:59,500
Rym: But I feel like the social media itself is tailored to a bunch of different outcomes that aren't designed with kids in mind.

217
00:19:00,341 --> 00:19:11,360
Scott: The one that is the concern of the Surgeon General is not the Chinese trying to convince people who to vote for, especially teens who cannot vote yet.

218
00:19:11,920 --> 00:19:20,340
Rym: What's tricky though is that we could argue these exact same effects come from advertising and we don't put warnings on advertising.

219
00:19:21,921 --> 00:19:30,700
Scott: Speaking of which, very small news story, you know, I keep saying this a lot and it's like, there's a place in the world called Scotland.

220
00:19:31,681 --> 00:19:39,520
Scott: And it was haha funny because my name was Scott and then it was haha because that's where, you know, Scrooge McDuck is from.

221
00:19:40,601 --> 00:19:42,639
Scott: And they have the kilts and the haggis, right?

222
00:19:43,081 --> 00:19:50,760
Scott: But later in life, everyone I met from Scotland, right, was just, I don't, just like the awesomest person, right?

223
00:19:51,041 --> 00:19:58,940
Scott: And I started to get this impression that like, wow, Scotland must actually be like a kick-ass kind of place because everyone I know from there is kind of cool, right?

224
00:19:59,241 --> 00:20:01,292
Scott: I've never met like a bad Scottish person.

225
00:20:01,312 --> 00:20:02,760
Scott: They were always like, great.

226
00:20:03,361 --> 00:20:12,080
Scott: And then when you see, when you learn every time Scotland comes up, it's always like, oh, that guy who came to the, to the New York Rangers read it by accident.

227
00:20:12,400 --> 00:20:16,728
Scott: It was the soccer team, the Rangers from Scotland, right?

228
00:20:16,748 --> 00:20:22,218
Scott: Or, uh, you know, them chanting at the soccer game, uh, Lizzie's in a box, right?

229
00:20:22,238 --> 00:20:23,340
Scott: That was funny, right?

230
00:20:23,581 --> 00:20:26,549
Scott: It's like, I just never see anything bad about Scotland.

231
00:20:26,609 --> 00:20:28,535
Scott: and you know what Scott Edinburgh, right?

232
00:20:28,856 --> 00:20:30,420
Scott: I hope I'm pronouncing it close to correctly.

233
00:20:30,460 --> 00:20:30,701
Scott: Edinburgh?

234
00:20:31,343 --> 00:20:33,852
Scott: I know it's, it's not Edin, you don't say Edinburgh.

235
00:20:33,912 --> 00:20:36,199
Scott: I know that it's like Edinburgh or something like that.

236
00:20:36,921 --> 00:20:39,512
Scott: Uh, sorry, amazing Scottish people, right?

237
00:20:39,613 --> 00:20:40,336
Scott: I just hyped you up.

238
00:20:40,376 --> 00:20:41,059
Scott: Don't be mad at me.

239
00:20:42,902 --> 00:20:51,260
Scott: Uh, that city that is difficult to pronounce with, uh, my tongue, uh, they just banned ads for SUVs among other things.

240
00:20:53,121 --> 00:20:53,664
Scott: How about that?

241
00:20:54,085 --> 00:20:57,860
Scott: I mean, you're not allowed to advertise SUVs in that city.

242
00:20:58,061 --> 00:21:02,695
Rym: A big part of reducing smoking in America wasn't warning labels or any of that stuff.

243
00:21:02,735 --> 00:21:04,280
Rym: It was just banning cigarette ads on TV.

244
00:21:04,820 --> 00:21:06,106
Scott: Banning cigarette ads is huge.

245
00:21:06,287 --> 00:21:07,514
Scott: We should ban alcohol ads.

246
00:21:07,674 --> 00:21:08,720
Scott: We should ban car ads.

247
00:21:09,862 --> 00:21:12,231
Scott: We should ban ads for pretty much anything that that's harmful.

248
00:21:12,251 --> 00:21:14,400
Scott: Pharmacy, uh, you know, pharmaceutical drugs.

249
00:21:15,620 --> 00:21:19,310
Rym: I mean, pharmaceutical drugs weren't like, they were banned.

250
00:21:19,330 --> 00:21:23,040
Rym: They were unbanned while we were alive and we watched everything get worse.

251
00:21:23,741 --> 00:21:31,860
Rym: But, uh, but the other thing that stands out to me is that this is a fundamentally new frontier because when we were kids, there was internet, but only for special kids.

252
00:21:32,282 --> 00:21:34,189
Rym: And there wasn't social media yet.

253
00:21:34,209 --> 00:21:36,860
Rym: Like Orkut appeared when we were already in college.

254
00:21:37,401 --> 00:21:47,420
Rym: So when I was in high school, like in middle school, the peak time of bullying and body shaming and all the stuff that's being rapidly accelerated by modern social media.

255
00:21:49,480 --> 00:21:54,995
Rym: The means of communicating on the internet was like this invisible secret world that only the cool kids were on.

256
00:21:55,035 --> 00:21:56,900
Rym: It was almost like an escape from reality.

257
00:21:57,500 --> 00:22:04,200
Scott: And I think everyone, most people in my high school had AOL or could instant message that by that means.

258
00:22:05,020 --> 00:22:08,539
Rym: I would say only about half the kids at my high school actually had AIM and used it.

259
00:22:09,361 --> 00:22:11,580
Scott: I guess, you know, it was some significant number though.

260
00:22:11,720 --> 00:22:14,199
Rym: And the kids that were on IRC was like only the D&D nerds.

261
00:22:14,580 --> 00:22:15,342
Scott: Oh, no one was on.

262
00:22:15,442 --> 00:22:16,264
Scott: Oh no, forget that.

263
00:22:16,325 --> 00:22:22,780
Scott: No, but you know, there were kids who were not nerdy kids who I was able to like dial up command and conquer with.

264
00:22:23,942 --> 00:22:27,432
Rym: But not that many, like the internet communication was largely separate.

265
00:22:27,693 --> 00:22:30,060
Rym: There wasn't just like, you only had the contacts of like specific people.

266
00:22:30,060 --> 00:22:34,798
Scott: There wasn't a social media feed that was in your phone that you could use all the time, every day for free.

267
00:22:35,660 --> 00:22:37,064
Rym: And none of it was public.

268
00:22:37,104 --> 00:22:43,260
Rym: It was always with photos, you know, it was always just basically direct messaging or occasionally group chats.

269
00:22:43,401 --> 00:22:45,920
Rym: Like it was just a fundamentally different environment.

270
00:22:46,341 --> 00:23:00,140
Rym: And one sentiment that I've seen in a lot of these articles related to this topic with teens specifically is that social media has made it to where, whereas before that they'd go to school, they'd get bullied because bullying has always been a problem in American schools.

271
00:23:00,582 --> 00:23:04,360
Rym: They go home, they're safe and free until they go back to the school.

272
00:23:04,981 --> 00:23:08,210
Rym: But now it follows them home on social media.

273
00:23:08,331 --> 00:23:11,320
Rym: and social media is how they interact with all their real friends and they're kind of just stuck.

274
00:23:12,381 --> 00:23:13,784
Rym: Like it's very hard to escape that.

275
00:23:14,105 --> 00:23:19,318
Rym: And if they stop using it, then they lose all their social contact and it's almost just as bad in a different direction.

276
00:23:19,338 --> 00:23:20,000
Rym: They get isolated.

277
00:23:21,441 --> 00:23:22,462
Rym: I don't know what to do about it.

278
00:23:23,765 --> 00:23:36,446
Rym: I don't think the warning labels will do anything, but I think they're still important because the warning labels on cigarettes and the warning labels on alcohol and like these kinds of things, they're a signal where society at least agrees.

279
00:23:36,887 --> 00:23:38,670
Rym: We agree that this is a problem.

280
00:23:39,491 --> 00:23:44,620
Rym: Even if we don't agree on the specific nature of the problem or what to do about it, we at least agree there's a problem here.

281
00:23:44,980 --> 00:23:51,280
Rym: Like it's not a leading indicator, but it's a trailing indicator of people are paying more attention to this.

282
00:23:52,821 --> 00:23:53,101
Scott: Yeah.

283
00:23:53,141 --> 00:24:05,580
Scott: You know, it's sort of like the, uh, you know, the ESRB ratings on the video games rated R and the movies and the, you know, it's like, you know, I think those things haven't been proven to be harmful.

284
00:24:06,260 --> 00:24:06,541
Scott: Right.

285
00:24:06,601 --> 00:24:12,589
Scott: So it's a little bit different, but it's, it's sort of just like, Hey, more information can't hurt, especially if it's true.

286
00:24:12,609 --> 00:24:19,700
Scott: Um, and it might take, you know, make someone at least think twice or, you know, be like, Hey, what?

287
00:24:19,961 --> 00:24:20,342
Scott: Yeah.

288
00:24:20,885 --> 00:24:24,119
Scott: Whereas before they didn't think anything of it and now they might think something of it.

289
00:24:24,540 --> 00:24:24,600
Rym: Yup.

290
00:24:24,941 --> 00:24:29,648
Rym: And it's easy for like, I don't want to disregard the danger of modern social media for kids.

291
00:24:29,668 --> 00:24:32,273
Rym: Cause again, that didn't exist when we were kids.

292
00:24:32,333 --> 00:24:36,420
Rym: Like the internet was a very different place when we were like using the internet as teens.

293
00:24:38,561 --> 00:24:41,665
Rym: So in some other news, uh, I was unaware of this cause.

294
00:24:41,706 --> 00:24:42,487
Rym: it's not affecting me.

295
00:24:42,587 --> 00:24:47,374
Rym: Apparently 13th and 14th gen, uh, Intel i9s.

296
00:24:47,875 --> 00:24:50,719
Rym: So Raptor Lake and Raptor Lake S like them, like I have one of those.

297
00:24:50,739 --> 00:24:51,059
Rym: I have an i9.

298
00:24:52,361 --> 00:24:56,730
Rym: Uh, people are, there's, there are instability issues and crashes that people are experiencing.

299
00:24:57,391 --> 00:24:59,075
Rym: And this has been going on for months.

300
00:24:59,235 --> 00:25:01,360
Rym: I only learned about it, uh, from this article.

301
00:25:02,381 --> 00:25:15,320
Rym: Uh, and the funniest thing about this entire situation is that while there is no official statement on the root cause, uh, it has to do with some algorithms, uh, that Intel says they might make a fix about.

302
00:25:15,740 --> 00:25:29,340
Rym: But it seems like the only people who experienced this ever are people who have increased the voltage they're sending to their CPU above spec, meaning anyone who turns on that automatic overclocking bullshit in their motherboard.

303
00:25:30,081 --> 00:25:31,402
Scott: Well, here's the thing though, right?

304
00:25:31,863 --> 00:25:36,568
Scott: If you're not going to turn on the overclocking or whatever, right?

305
00:25:37,008 --> 00:25:39,972
Scott: Cause as far as I know, I could be wrong on this, right?

306
00:25:40,432 --> 00:25:47,420
Scott: But as far as I'm aware, uh, an i9 and i7 are like nearly the same thing, right?

307
00:25:47,500 --> 00:25:53,648
Scott: I think there's like a few more cores and more threads on the i9 and like a little less gigahertz.

308
00:25:54,169 --> 00:26:02,159
Scott: And actually like in, if you look at like benchmark websites, like i9 is like 1% effective speed faster than the i7, right?

309
00:26:03,280 --> 00:26:06,085
Scott: Yet it costs like $160 more, which is like, you know, like 50% more money.

310
00:26:06,145 --> 00:26:09,410
Scott: So you're paying 50% more money for an i9 over an i7 and getting 1% overall benefit from that.

311
00:26:15,880 --> 00:26:18,208
Scott: It's really not a good value to buy an i9.

312
00:26:18,268 --> 00:26:22,000
Scott: It's, it's a, it's just people who have to have the biggest number.

313
00:26:22,281 --> 00:26:24,939
Rym: Well, it's actually even more specific because...

314
00:26:25,120 --> 00:26:32,391
Scott: But the thing is with the i9 is that if you do, right, do some big time overclocking, right?

315
00:26:32,511 --> 00:26:38,260
Scott: Like you said, the i9 is theoretically supposed to be able to withstand the overclocking.

316
00:26:38,461 --> 00:26:38,682
Rym: Yes.

317
00:26:38,742 --> 00:26:42,239
Rym: If you do big time, like I'm going to overclock, then it's fine.

318
00:26:43,160 --> 00:26:43,280
Scott: Right.

319
00:26:43,300 --> 00:26:52,094
Scott: That's the only way to get the benefit of your extra, you know, $160 or whatever it is extra is to overclock the i9.

320
00:26:52,194 --> 00:26:54,858
Scott: If you're not going to overclock it, you might as well get an i7.

321
00:26:54,898 --> 00:26:56,200
Scott: You're just wasting money if you don't.

322
00:26:56,960 --> 00:27:07,740
Rym: But the narrow issue here seems to be around the fact that the core, the i9 will throttle its turbo speed down once it hits critical temperatures.

323
00:27:08,081 --> 00:27:12,119
Rym: So you'll get insane bursts of speed until you hit a critical temperature and then it backs off.

324
00:27:13,180 --> 00:27:28,700
Rym: And basically people who override that to make it throttle back less cause elevated temperatures repeatedly, which the cause due to cumulative exposure to elevated core voltages cause the problem in the algorithm and cause the thing to crash.

325
00:27:29,681 --> 00:27:31,848
Rym: So people who fully overclock are not having the problem.

326
00:27:32,390 --> 00:27:35,080
Rym: It's people who don't do anything weird are not having the problem.

327
00:27:35,421 --> 00:27:39,694
Rym: It's people who use their motherboard bullshit, but don't know what they're doing and just click on the button.

328
00:27:39,734 --> 00:27:41,560
Rym: That's like, yeah, make my thing faster.

329
00:27:42,601 --> 00:27:46,929
Scott: Well, I think the, you know, the problem there is that, you know, the overclocking.

330
00:27:46,969 --> 00:27:52,200
Scott: basically you shouldn't be able to override the, you know, the, the protection mechanism.

331
00:27:52,260 --> 00:27:52,420
Scott: Right.

332
00:27:52,520 --> 00:28:00,920
Scott: It's like there's, and in fact, that's another question is like, why do you even need to like expose these settings at all?

333
00:28:00,980 --> 00:28:01,100
Scott: Right.

334
00:28:01,200 --> 00:28:18,320
Scott: Why can't it just intelligently, you know, check its temperature, which it knows, and then just keep increasing its own voltage and, and clock to whatever it can until it detects like, okay, this is as fast as I can go without causing it might without killing myself.

335
00:28:18,661 --> 00:28:24,777
Scott: And if you add more cooling, you know, and you put like a dry ice near it or something, it'll just be like, oh, I can go whee.

336
00:28:24,797 --> 00:28:25,198
Scott: Right.

337
00:28:25,940 --> 00:28:27,923
Scott: And you don't, you shouldn't have to do any settings.

338
00:28:27,983 --> 00:28:39,440
Scott: It should all be intelligent and automatic and there shouldn't be a manual way to even improve the settings because the automatic way should in theory, just always do better than you could do with manual settings.

339
00:28:39,440 --> 00:28:41,626
Rym: Think about old Intel chips.

340
00:28:41,646 --> 00:28:45,638
Rym: Remember all the nerds are always mad that Intel was like locked, like you can't change the multipliers.

341
00:28:45,698 --> 00:28:46,300
Rym: You can't configure.

342
00:28:46,340 --> 00:28:47,665
Scott: Some of them you could, some of them you couldn't.

343
00:28:47,685 --> 00:28:48,749
Rym: Most of them you couldn't back in the day.

344
00:28:48,769 --> 00:28:51,640
Rym: I'm talking like Pentium one, Pentium two, Pentium three.

345
00:28:52,440 --> 00:28:58,748
Rym: And then when Intel started allowing this, people would pry their piece, like the overclocking world got kind of easy.

346
00:28:58,788 --> 00:29:08,160
Rym: And then a lot of people made a lot of mistakes, but I think Intel originally people would overclock because Intel would be bidding chips and then locking them.

347
00:29:08,581 --> 00:29:13,452
Rym: But you might get lucky and have a chip that says it's 400 megahertz, but could really go 600 megahertz.

348
00:29:13,913 --> 00:29:16,920
Rym: But that's not the situation with modern overclocking.

349
00:29:17,180 --> 00:29:18,843
Rym: It's a much more nuanced thing.

350
00:29:18,863 --> 00:29:24,415
Rym: And I don't feel like the average consumer understands anything about those voltages enough to do anything with them.

351
00:29:24,675 --> 00:29:26,820
Rym: And they're kind of just playing around trial and error.

352
00:29:27,441 --> 00:29:41,820
Scott: Well, I think that also is that the vast majority of people who are buying these sort of ridiculous high end, but still consumer available hardware are people who are just playing with their computer and not actually using their computer.

353
00:29:42,180 --> 00:29:42,300
Scott: Right.

354
00:29:42,621 --> 00:29:50,120
Scott: It's like those people who are like, you know, have a knife collection and sharpen all their knives and it, you know, that has value, I guess, but they don't cook anything.

355
00:29:50,681 --> 00:29:50,881
Scott: Right.

356
00:29:51,222 --> 00:29:58,216
Scott: And it's like these people, it's like their hobby is fuck around with computer and not actually use computer to do anything.

357
00:29:58,416 --> 00:29:58,597
Scott: Right.

358
00:29:58,697 --> 00:30:00,380
Scott: Other than maybe play a video game.

359
00:30:00,440 --> 00:30:06,639
Scott: But maybe when they play a video game, most of the time they're just looking at the frame rate and not actually playing the video game, even with ridiculous.

360
00:30:07,420 --> 00:30:10,005
Scott: So the fact that there are settings to change.

361
00:30:10,386 --> 00:30:10,566
Scott: Right.

362
00:30:10,606 --> 00:30:14,254
Scott: The fact that there are settings to change like that is the thing.

363
00:30:14,314 --> 00:30:15,276
Scott: It's like they just want they.

364
00:30:15,596 --> 00:30:17,500
Scott: they enjoy the changing of settings.

365
00:30:17,600 --> 00:30:29,500
Scott: If you did, if you made an automatic thing that just did it better than they could and there was no settings to change, then you wouldn't sell to those people because they'd be like, you know, that would take there'd be no nothing for them to enjoy.

366
00:30:29,760 --> 00:30:33,852
Scott: They just like, oh, I bought the best computer and it's automatically as fast as it can possibly be.

367
00:30:33,872 --> 00:30:34,775
Scott: And I can't do any better.

368
00:30:35,056 --> 00:30:36,440
Scott: And there's no settings necessary.

369
00:30:37,481 --> 00:30:42,958
Scott: And then they sit there and they benchmark it and they say, OK, and then what do they do?

370
00:30:43,821 --> 00:30:46,766
Scott: It's like they had to use the computer to do something, but they don't do that.

371
00:30:47,026 --> 00:30:48,829
Scott: That's why benchmarking is their hobby.

372
00:30:48,869 --> 00:30:51,394
Rym: I've never run a benchmark on this workstation because I don't care.

373
00:30:51,434 --> 00:30:54,900
Rym: You know, my benchmark was I edited a premiere thing and I exported it.

374
00:30:55,000 --> 00:30:55,963
Rym: And was it fast enough?

375
