Rym: So it's designed so that a cop shouldn't be able to say, give me your phone so I can look at your ID.

774
00:59:18,309 --> 00:59:19,651
Rym: They have to use the different process.

775
00:59:20,392 --> 00:59:25,260
Rym: I am 0% confident that cops will not just fucking rip your phone out of your hand as soon as you open the app.

776
00:59:26,640 --> 00:59:26,740
Scott: Yeah.

777
00:59:26,760 --> 00:59:36,640
Scott: I mean, theoretically, the way it should work is they should tap, you know, your phone should be locked and they should, you know, tap their device on your device, which would be possible if they used the app.

778
00:59:36,740 --> 00:59:46,459
Scott: I mean, I guess it's theoretically possible to do that with their app, but it's much easier and much more possible if it was integrated into the wallet.

779
00:59:47,540 --> 00:59:55,050
Rym: So the place we ski, the pass we get, they did introduce an app for phone passes and it is just always on without the app and it does just work.

780
00:59:56,232 --> 00:59:59,977
Rym: But, uh, the phones just don't get detected most of the time.

781
01:00:00,017 --> 01:00:02,320
Rym: So I ended up disabling the app and getting a physical card anyway.

782
01:00:02,941 --> 01:00:03,121
Scott: Yeah.

783
01:00:03,321 --> 01:00:10,410
Scott: Omni does it, you know, the way Omni works, the way the Apple transit works is like your phone can actually like have a dead battery on.

784
01:00:10,470 --> 01:00:19,060
Scott: like, you know, and it would still, you can, the phone could be off with a dead battery and you could tap Omni and it'll most likely still beep and let you through the turnstile.

785
01:00:19,741 --> 01:00:23,389
Scott: So there's no reason the ID function couldn't work the same way.

786
01:00:23,409 --> 01:00:26,676
Scott: Um, they just didn't do it that way in New York.

787
01:00:26,816 --> 01:00:28,360
Scott: Other States did it the official way.

788
01:00:28,620 --> 01:00:38,692
Rym: But, uh, this idea is fucking worthless because there is currently no requirement for law enforcement or any establishment to treat this as a real ID.

789
01:00:38,712 --> 01:00:40,073
Rym: There's no timeline for that.

790
01:00:40,334 --> 01:00:41,575
Rym: There's no deadline for that.

791
01:00:42,036 --> 01:00:45,700
Rym: acceptance, including by the police is quote voluntary.

792
01:00:46,820 --> 01:00:51,645
Rym: So I am, so you can't use, I still have to carry my driver's license if I'm in a car.

793
01:00:51,665 --> 01:00:54,308
Scott: Yeah, we can't get rid of our wallets just yet.

794
01:00:54,508 --> 01:00:58,452
Rym: Well, at some point I have found I do still need physical credit cards.

795
01:00:58,492 --> 01:01:04,979
Rym: There are a lot of restaurants I've been to recently that don't, they have to take your credit card back and swipe it.

796
01:01:05,019 --> 01:01:06,080
Rym: They don't have a way for you to tap.

797
01:01:07,001 --> 01:01:07,141
Scott: Yep.

798
01:01:07,421 --> 01:01:19,419
Scott: There are places like that, but it's, I think it's fewer because you know, any place that buys a new point of sale system will have, you know, they're not selling point of sale systems that don't tap anymore.

799
01:01:19,439 --> 01:01:19,860
Scott: Right?

800
01:01:20,240 --> 01:01:32,020
Scott: So you'd have to, if you want it to not take taps, you'd have to like simply just refuse even though you could because you're weird for some reason, uh, or buy like an old cash register on eBay.

801
01:01:32,020 --> 01:01:35,424
Scott: And it goes, ding, you know, something like that.

802
01:01:35,744 --> 01:01:43,774
Scott: But you know, it's mostly businesses that have already existed already have equipment and haven't upgraded it that are not taking any.

803
01:01:44,034 --> 01:01:48,700
Scott: And I guess the point at which their equipment stops working is when they'll buy something new.

804
01:01:50,240 --> 01:01:50,821
Scott: Yep.

805
01:01:51,002 --> 01:01:52,023
Rym: Or just go cash only.

806
01:01:53,546 --> 01:01:53,926
Scott: Who knows?

807
01:01:54,607 --> 01:01:54,948
Rym: All right.

808
01:01:55,008 --> 01:02:03,040
Rym: So, uh, last two little news is, uh, so you might, there was a little bit of a kerfuffle when Microsoft showed off, uh, windows recall.

809
01:02:03,840 --> 01:02:16,799
Scott: And so if you don't know, windows recall was basically the system that was going to record everything you do on your computer base, almost everything you do in your computer and then allow you to sort of roll back and undo like everything.

810
01:02:16,819 --> 01:02:17,260
Scott: Right?

811
01:02:17,440 --> 01:02:20,985
Scott: So it's sort of like, or see what you did in the past.

812
01:02:21,165 --> 01:02:23,348
Scott: It could be a really useful and powerful feature.

813
01:02:24,009 --> 01:02:32,660
Scott: Um, but it's also a dangerous feature because if someone accesses your computer, they could just scroll back and see some prod or whatever, who knows what, right.

814
01:02:33,020 --> 01:02:34,843
Scott: You know, um, not great.

815
01:02:35,304 --> 01:02:47,160
Scott: And so people were upset about this from a security standpoint because they're like, Hey, if someone breaks into your computer, it's like, now they got all this, you know, a mate, it's way more valuable to them to once they've got in.

816
01:02:47,240 --> 01:02:49,704
Scott: Because they can access all this recall stuff that's been recorded.

817
01:02:49,765 --> 01:02:55,114
Scott: And I'm like, listen, you know, if I'm going to complain about security, I'm complaining about the lock.

818
01:02:55,355 --> 01:02:58,360
Scott: I'm not complaining about how much treasure is in the chest, right?

819
01:02:58,660 --> 01:03:00,402
Scott: Adding more treasure to the chest.

820
01:03:00,763 --> 01:03:03,466
Scott: Doesn't make a security problem necessarily.

821
01:03:03,867 --> 01:03:05,349
Scott: If the lock is strong enough, right?

822
01:03:05,369 --> 01:03:10,536
Scott: You just have to have a lock that is proportional to the treasure, but the lock is what it's always been.

823
01:03:11,117 --> 01:03:13,500
Scott: Windows is as secured as, as it is.

824
01:03:13,861 --> 01:03:14,082
Rym: Yep.

825
01:03:14,644 --> 01:03:18,700
Rym: There's a reason my PC is not accessible directly over the internet and never will be.

826
01:03:19,380 --> 01:03:19,460
Scott: Yeah.

827
01:03:19,480 --> 01:03:26,410
Scott: It's like they just added more treasure without, and it's like, okay, you know, um, there is maybe something to be concerned about.

828
01:03:26,630 --> 01:03:34,040
Scott: And also they had the feature on by default, which is also, you know, a well, and the fact that people might not even realize that it's there.

829
01:03:34,260 --> 01:03:39,000
Rym: They were branding it heavily as part of Co-Pilot Plus and there's, it feels like...

830
01:03:39,020 --> 01:03:39,140
Scott: Right.

831
01:03:39,160 --> 01:03:39,721
Scott: Because what, right.

832
01:03:39,741 --> 01:03:42,968
Scott: Because basically the Co-Pilot AI would be looking at that.

833
01:03:43,028 --> 01:03:47,657
Scott: And when you asked it questions, it would have the context of all your computer history to help you.

834
01:03:47,697 --> 01:03:49,420
Scott: And that is a powerful feature.

835
01:03:49,700 --> 01:04:07,540
Rym: And I will note for everyone who's mad about machine learning, which is way beyond the scope of this episode, the safest possible machine learning is where you have generic models provided by companies, but they run locally against data that is not sent anywhere and are actually yours.

836
01:04:08,401 --> 01:04:08,561
Scott: Yep.

837
01:04:08,901 --> 01:04:21,095
Scott: I mean, that's what Apple announced at WWDC is that most of their machine Apple intelligence is going to be local, but some of it can't be local because your local isn't powerful enough.

838
01:04:21,195 --> 01:04:25,660
Scott: And in that case, yeah, if they, you have to give permission to send it to out.

839
01:04:26,180 --> 01:04:32,988
Scott: And if you do give it permission, the code that does the sending is public and everyone can see and verify that.

840
01:04:33,048 --> 01:04:38,453
Scott: it's impossible for Apple or anyone to actually learn anything about you.

841
01:04:38,693 --> 01:04:38,874
Scott: Right.

842
01:04:38,894 --> 01:04:44,940
Scott: You're just basically using their, you know, so theoretically, but you can always just say no and only use local too.

843
01:04:45,220 --> 01:04:45,421
Rym: Yeah.

844
01:04:45,441 --> 01:04:47,183
Rym: And the thing is local.

845
01:04:47,483 --> 01:04:51,569
Rym: machine learning is going to be something that could radically transform the world.

846
01:04:51,649 --> 01:04:57,437
Rym: But this is very difficult to do in an era where nobody has workstations anymore.

847
01:04:57,998 --> 01:04:59,500
Rym: And like everyone's device is a phone.

848
01:04:59,680 --> 01:05:00,999
Scott: You can't run a model on a phone.

849
01:05:01,901 --> 01:05:03,950
Scott: Your phone can do enough, right?

850
01:05:04,292 --> 01:05:05,959
Scott: It's a surprising amount the phone can do.

851
01:05:06,260 --> 01:05:08,320
Rym: Well, it depends on the scale of algorithm you want.

852
01:05:08,540 --> 01:05:13,660
Scott: And you look at Apple throwing more and more ridiculous processors into their phones and tablets.

853
01:05:14,421 --> 01:05:19,166
Rym: I do feel like we might, that the data cube or the processing cube will come back someday.

854
01:05:19,226 --> 01:05:31,320
Rym: I still think there'll be a future where people somewhere in their home have effectively a workstation that's just a black box that they plug in and forget about and all the devices use it as processing power and data link and whatever.

855
01:05:33,201 --> 01:05:37,811
Scott: I can't see a normal person just going and buying like this box.

856
01:05:37,992 --> 01:05:41,660
Scott: They just plug it in their house and setting it up and then like.

857
01:05:41,840 --> 01:05:49,294
Rym: Well, what if the box is sold as this box will spy on your family and all your devices until it learns you.

858
01:05:49,375 --> 01:05:52,000
Rym: And then it is a human sounding sentient.

859
01:05:53,101 --> 01:06:00,896
Scott: But it's like if it doesn't have its own interface, I don't think people see value in it as it just like, you know, in the way that it allows their phone to do something else.

860
01:06:01,036 --> 01:06:02,720
Scott: It's a really tough sell to normal people.

861
01:06:05,581 --> 01:06:07,066
Rym: Ah, so last little news.

862
01:06:08,490 --> 01:06:11,400
Rym: Voyager 1 started sending data back again.

863
01:06:12,280 --> 01:06:18,533
Scott: Yeah, those geniuses figure out somehow to make this old piece of junk keep working even though it's super far away.

864
01:06:19,014 --> 01:06:22,240
Scott: Man, the people who engineered that in the first place sure got a lot of things right.

865
01:06:23,002 --> 01:06:29,477
Rym: Yeah, 47 years it's been hurtling away from Earth as one of the fastest things that humans have ever made.

866
01:06:30,138 --> 01:06:30,960
Rym: And it's still going.

867
01:06:31,780 --> 01:06:32,442
Scott: It's still going.

868
01:06:32,562 --> 01:06:33,505
Scott: It's still functioning.

869
01:06:33,525 --> 01:06:38,919
Scott: They can still talk to it and get it to do things even though it's like way out there.

870
01:06:39,380 --> 01:06:54,660
Rym: Yup, like for all the fucked up nonsense that we just talked about in the tech news, I think we should end on Voyager 1 because every now and then us angry violent monkeys will somehow do something that's actually cool and good for everybody.

871
01:06:55,620 --> 01:06:59,720
Scott: It's basically just like a satellite dish and like some instruments holding on.

872
01:07:00,281 --> 01:07:02,448
Rym: Four instruments holding on, sending back.

873
01:07:03,089 --> 01:07:06,440
Rym: It is currently more than 15 billion miles away from Earth.

874
01:07:07,422 --> 01:07:08,329
Scott: Mhm, mhm.

875
01:07:09,115 --> 01:07:09,679
Scott: That's pretty far.

